Hyperemesis Gravidarum
Obstetrics Gynaecology & Womens health
Intractable vomiting, >5% weight loss, Ketonuria/metabolic alkalosis

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: OGW-018
Reason for Visit: Intractable vomiting causing acute dehydration, orthostatic presyncope, and absolute inability to tolerate oral fluids for 24 hours.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and husband
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated continuous, intractable vomiting in a first-trimester patient resulting in complete inability to hydrate and recurrent dizziness on standing. Urgent assessment was required to administer parenteral antiemetics and assess clinical hydration status to prevent severe hypovolemic shock and electrolyte collapse before the next available in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I have thrown up over twenty times today, I cannot even keep a sip of water down, and my chest is pounding.
History of Present Illness: Symptoms began progressively two weeks ago but severely escalated 24 hours ago. The patient, currently at 9 weeks gestation, reports intractable nausea and vomiting presenting early in the first trimester. She reports an absolute inability to tolerate any oral food or fluids. She documents a weight loss exceeding 5 percent of her pre-pregnancy weight (down 3.5kg). Tonight, she developed severe orthostatic presyncope upon attempting to stand, accompanied by a resting tachycardia. She complains of continuous ptyalism (excessive salivation), generalized fatigue, and profound weakness.
Functional Status: Bedbound; unable to ambulate to the bathroom without assistance due to dizziness.
Social/Safety Concerns: Husband is exhausted; risk of severe maternal dehydration and acute kidney injury.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 92/58 mmHg (supine) | HR: 118 bpm | Temp: 36.6 C | RR: 16 bpm | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 58 Kg
General: Patient appears lethargic, pale, with sunken eyes and dry lips, holding an emesis basin.
Physical Exam: Examination reveals clear signs of severe dehydration including dry mucous membranes, absent saliva pooling, and poor skin turgor (delayed tenting). Cardiovascular exam shows a regular, rapid resting tachycardia without murmurs. Abdomen is completely soft, scaphoid, with no localized tenderness, guarding, or peritonism. A distinct ketotic breath odor (fruity/acetone) is immediately noticeable. Point-of-care urinalysis demonstrates 3+ starvation ketonuria, specific gravity >1.030, but is negative for leukocytes or nitrites.
Environmental Observations: Emesis basin containing small amounts of bile-stained fluid; dark, quiet room.
Medication Reconciliation: Attempted oral doxylamine and pyridoxine yesterday, completely vomited.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Severe Hyperemesis Gravidarum with acute dehydration and ketosis - Status: Deteriorating
Differentials:

Acute Gastroenteritis - Ruled out by the complete absence of diarrhea, lack of fever, and the protracted, progressive first-trimester timeline.

Acute Appendicitis - Ruled out by the soft, entirely non-tender abdomen without localized right lower quadrant pain.

Urinary Tract Infection / Pyelonephritis - Ruled out by the absence of dysuria, negative nitrites/leukocytes on urinalysis, and lack of costovertebral angle tenderness.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with classic, severe hyperemesis gravidarum complicated by objective signs of significant clinical dehydration (resting tachycardia, hypotension, poor turgor) and profound starvation ketonuria. The complete inability to tolerate oral fluids for 24 hours created an imminent risk of hypovolemic shock and severe hypokalemic hypochloremic metabolic alkalosis. Urgent after-hours assessment was clinically mandated to administer immediate intramuscular antiemetics and to formally assess whether immediate emergency ambulance transfer for intravenous fluid resuscitation was required, an intervention that absolutely could not be delayed until the next in-hours period.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered intramuscular ondansetron 8mg and intramuscular metoclopramide 10mg. After 30 minutes, the patient was able to tolerate 50mL of oral rehydration solution without emesis. Prescribed sublingual ondansetron 4mg every 8 hours. Initiated an urgent referral to the hospital Hospital in the Home (HITH) program for daily intravenous hydration and electrolyte replacement.
Positive Behavioral Change: To maintain hydration, take only one tiny sip of electrolyte solution every 10 minutes rather than large gulps; experiencing the absence of a vomiting reflex will reinforce the habit of micro-dosing fluids. To prevent sudden nausea spikes, avoid brushing your teeth immediately after eating or drinking; recognizing the reduction in the gag reflex will validate this temporary hygiene adjustment.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Urgent referral to Hospital in the Home (HITH) for IV fluids.
DME/Safety (Optional): Not applicable.
Follow-up: Must be seen by the HITH nursing team or regular GP within 12 hours for ongoing fluid management.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present immediately to the Emergency Department if the vomiting resumes and you cannot keep any fluids down for another 6 hours, if you become confused or extremely drowsy, or if you stop passing urine entirely for 8 hours.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The injections are a temporary measure to stop the vomiting cycle; you require professional intravenous hydration tomorrow.
Clinical Course: The nausea should be blunted for the next 6 to 8 hours. You will likely feel exhausted as your body recovers from the dehydration.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate emergency care if the severe vomiting restarts, or if you stop producing urine.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The severe pregnancy hormones have triggered a loop in your brain causing constant vomiting, leading your body to burn fat for energy (creating the ketones in your urine) and become severely dehydrated. The strong injections block the nausea center in your brain, breaking the cycle so you can slowly start absorbing water again.
Actionable Steps: Take the sublingual wafer under your tongue exactly on schedule, do not wait for the nausea to return. Sip the electrolyte solution incredibly slowly tonight.

